  <div class="story">
    <div><h3>The problem</h3><p>The agency sells two things to two different people. A brand wants proof that a creator's community is the right audience before paying for a campaign. A creator wants to know the agency will build them a business, with or without the next algorithm change.</p><p>The old site treated both the same and could only be changed by a developer. It also had hundreds of indexed pages whose addresses could not be allowed to break.</p></div>
    <div><h3>What I built</h3><p>I designed the visual direction and built the whole site: a home page that explains the method with a diagram that draws as you scroll, a case-study explorer with video reels, and a talent directory with search, filters and sorting that live in the URL.</p><p>Behind it sits a content studio where the team builds any page from 20 block types and sees a live preview. I also built consent management with an exportable log, structured data for search engines and 504 redirects from the old addresses.</p></div>
    <div><h3>What made it hard</h3><p>The brand allows one background colour and white. With no second colour to lean on, every level of hierarchy had to come from type size, weight, outline and a few hand-drawn marks.</p><p>The motion had to stay honest too. The particle field, the pinned diagram and the smooth scrolling all run together, so each one is switched off or drawn in its finished state on phones, for reduced-motion users and without JavaScript.</p></div>
  </div>

